### Intro

I contribute to MuJoCo Warp, which runs MuJoCo's GJK/EPA in float32 with the constants of the `mjUSESINGLE` build, so I compare against MuJoCo C built with `mjUSESINGLE`.

### My setup

MuJoCo C at main (aa9a0eaef), built with CMake and `-DmjUSESINGLE` in `CMAKE_C_FLAGS` and `CMAKE_CXX_FLAGS`. Linux x86_64, GCC. Double precision builds of the same commit for comparison.

### What's happening? What did you expect?

Since 3bbf510d4 ("Improve NativeCCD numerical precision"), a stack of thin box-shaped mesh plates resting exactly aligned on a mesh slab no longer settles in the single precision build. The four corner contacts of a face pair, which should have the same depth and the normal (0, 0, 1), get normals tilted by 3e-4 to 5e-4 rad and depths that differ by up to about 100 µm (for example -172, -209, -286, -249 µm on the top pair at step 999). The plates rock, and CG needs 10 to 20 times more iterations.

Mean CG iterations over the second half of 2000 steps, and max z drift of any plate over that half:

| plates (8 mm thick) | double, main | single, 3bbf510d4^ | single, main |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 3 | 1.3, 0.000 mm | 1.3, 0.000 mm | 16.8, 0.018 mm |
| 4 | 1.8, 0.001 mm | 1.8, 0.001 mm | 26.4, 0.074 mm |
| 5 | 2.0, 0.001 mm | 2.0, 0.001 mm | 19.9, 0.156 mm |

Within 3bbf510d4, the change comes from the single precision `mjMINDIST2` and `mjMINDIST3`, lowered from 1e-10f to 1e-17f: setting them back to 1e-10f restores equal depths, the (0, 0, 1) normal and 1 to 2 iterations. Reverting them is not a fix, though, because the lower values are what made shallow penetration work in single precision: with 1e-10f, `mj_geomDistance` returns 0 for aligned boxes or mesh boxes overlapping by 2 to 12 µm.

I have a candidate fix in `engine_collision_gjk.c` that keeps both and leaves double precision results unchanged, and can send it as a PR.

### Steps for reproduction

1. Build MuJoCo with `-DmjUSESINGLE`.
2. Compile the program below against it (with `-DmjUSESINGLE`) and run it on the model.
3. Compare with a double precision build, or with single precision at 3bbf510d4^.

### Minimal model for reproduction

```xml
<mujoco>
  <option timestep="0.002" solver="CG" cone="elliptic" impratio="10" integrator="implicitfast"
          iterations="100" ls_iterations="15" tolerance="1e-6"/>
  <asset>
    <mesh name="plate" vertex="-0.105 -0.148 -0.004  -0.105 -0.148 0.004  -0.105 0.148 -0.004  -0.105 0.148 0.004
                               0.105 -0.148 -0.004  0.105 -0.148 0.004  0.105 0.148 -0.004  0.105 0.148 0.004"/>
    <mesh name="slab" vertex="-0.3 -0.3 -0.02  -0.3 -0.3 0.02  -0.3 0.3 -0.02  -0.3 0.3 0.02
                              0.3 -0.3 -0.02  0.3 -0.3 0.02  0.3 0.3 -0.02  0.3 0.3 0.02"/>
  </asset>
  <worldbody>
    <body pos="0 0 0.02"><geom type="mesh" mesh="slab"/></body>
    <body pos="0 0 0.044"><freejoint/><geom type="mesh" mesh="plate" mass="0.05"/></body>
    <body pos="0 0 0.052"><freejoint/><geom type="mesh" mesh="plate" mass="0.05"/></body>
    <body pos="0 0 0.060"><freejoint/><geom type="mesh" mesh="plate" mass="0.05"/></body>
  </worldbody>
</mujoco>
```

### Code required for reproduction

```c
#include <math.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <mujoco/mujoco.h>

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
  char err[1000];
  mjModel* m = mj_loadXML(argv[1], NULL, err, sizeof(err));
  mjData* d = mj_makeData(m);
  double iters = 0, drift = 0, z0[3];
  for (int step = 0; step < 2000; step++) {
    mj_step(m, d);
    if (step == 999) {
      for (int c = 0; c < d->ncon; c++) {
        printf("geoms %d-%d dist %+.1f um normal (%.4f %.4f %.4f)\n", d->contact[c].geom[0], d->contact[c].geom[1],
               1e6 * d->contact[c].dist, d->contact[c].frame[0], d->contact[c].frame[1], d->contact[c].frame[2]);
      }
    }
    if (step >= 1000) {
      iters += d->solver_niter[0];
      for (int p = 0; p < 3; p++) {
        double z = d->qpos[7 * p + 2];
        if (step == 1000) z0[p] = z;
        drift = fmax(drift, fabs(z - z0[p]));
      }
    }
  }
  printf("mean CG iterations %.1f, max z drift %.3f mm\n", iters / 1000, 1e3 * drift);
  return 0;
}
```

